<h1>Kawhi Leonard <em>Hand Size &amp; Measurements</em></h1>
<div class="hero-meta">
<span class="hero-meta-item">📅 Updated July 15, 2026</span>
<span class="hero-meta-item">🏀 LA Clippers</span>
<span class="hero-meta-item">📊 Verified Combine Data</span>
</div>
<p class="hero-intro">
    Kawhi Leonard's hands measure 9.75 inches long and 11.25 inches wide — among the largest ever recorded at the NBA Draft Combine, and the physical trait that best explains a defensive résumé most players never approach. His trade back to Toronto has been agreed to since June 30 but remains on hold, frozen by an NBA investigation that could determine whether the deal ever goes through at all.
  </p>
</div><div class="article-wrap">
<div class="content">
<!-- SECTION 1 -->
<h2>The Verified Combine Measurements</h2>
<div class="player-card">
<div class="player-name">Kawhi Leonard</div>
<div class="player-sub">LA Clippers · Forward · 2x Finals MVP · 2x DPOY</div>
<div class="player-stats">
<div class="player-stat">
<div class="player-stat-val">6'6"</div>
<div class="player-stat-label">Height (barefoot)</div>
</div>
<div class="player-stat above">
<div class="player-stat-val">7'3"</div>
<div class="player-stat-label">Wingspan</div>
</div>
<div class="player-stat above">
<div class="player-stat-val">11.25"</div>
<div class="player-stat-label">Hand Width — near all-time top</div>
</div>
<div class="player-stat">
<div class="player-stat-val">32.0"</div>
<div class="player-stat-label">Max Vertical</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Leonard measured 6-foot-6 barefoot (6-foot-7 with shoes) and 227 pounds at the 2011 NBA Draft Combine, with a 7-foot-3 wingspan and an 8-foot-10 standing reach. None of those numbers are exceptional on their own — they are solid, prototypical small-forward measurements. His 32-inch max vertical and 11.45-second lane agility were similarly unremarkable; nothing at that combine predicted a two-time Finals MVP.</p>
<p>The one number that stood out was his hands. Hand length of 9.75 inches and hand width of 11.25 inches — measured from thumb tip to pinky tip with the hand outstretched — put him among a small handful of players in combine history with that level of grip. It's commonly cited as the third-largest hand span of any active NBA player, and it shows up constantly on film: strips that shouldn't be possible, one-handed control through contact, and a mid-range pull-up that looks unbothered by any degree of contest.</p>
<!-- SECTION 2 -->
<h2>The Underwhelming Combine That Became a Two-Time Finals MVP</h2>
<div class="rank-card">
<div class="rank-title">Leonard's 2011 Combine vs Typical Small Forward Prospect</div>
<div class="rank-row">
<div class="rank-label"><strong>Height</strong>6'6" barefoot</div>
<div class="rank-track"><div class="rank-fill" style="width:50%;background:#ffd600"></div></div>
<div class="rank-val">Average</div>
</div>
<div class="rank-row">
<div class="rank-label"><strong>Wingspan</strong>7'3"</div>
<div class="rank-track"><div class="rank-fill" style="width:58%;background:#ffd600"></div></div>
<div class="rank-val">Solid, unremarkable</div>
</div>
<div class="rank-row">
<div class="rank-label"><strong>Max Vertical</strong>32.0"</div>
<div class="rank-track"><div class="rank-fill" style="width:45%;background:#ff7000"></div></div>
<div class="rank-val">Below average</div>
</div>
<div class="rank-row">
<div class="rank-label"><strong>Hand Width</strong>11.25"</div>
<div class="rank-track"><div class="rank-fill" style="width:96%;background:#aaff00"></div></div>
<div class="rank-val">Elite, all-time rare</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Leonard slid to 15th overall in 2011 in part because nothing about his combine numbers screamed star. The Spurs traded for him on draft night anyway, betting on defensive instincts and shooting touch that testing doesn't capture. That bet paid off in 2014, when a 22-year-old Leonard took on prime LeBron James and won his first Finals MVP. A second followed in 2019 with Toronto, making him one of a small handful of players to win Finals MVP with two different franchises.</p>
<div class="callout">
<div class="callout-icon">✋</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>The hand-size pattern:</strong> Leonard's profile — wing-sized frame, oversized hands, defensive instincts that don't show up on a stat sheet — has become a scouting shorthand. Celtics rookie Hugo Gonzalez posted a 10.25-inch hand length at the 2025 combine, the second-longest ever measured, on a 6'6" frame. Scouts have already drawn the Kawhi comparison directly, for exactly this reason.</div>
</div>
