<tbody>
<tr class="highlight"><td>Raptors receive</td><td>Kawhi Leonard</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clippers receive</td><td>Brandon Ingram, Gradey Dick, unprotected 2031 &amp; 2033 first-round picks, 2027 first-round swap, two second-round picks</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The trade has not closed. Both teams announced it's on hold pending the outcome of an NBA investigation — open since last offseason — into whether the Clippers circumvented the salary cap through a $28 million endorsement deal between Leonard and Aspiration, a now-bankrupt company that also held a separate $300 million, 23-year sponsorship deal with the team itself. Clippers owner Steve Ballmer had personally invested in Aspiration and has denied knowledge of Leonard's specific deal.</p>
<p>The Raptors' statement was direct: the league told them that finalizing the trade before the investigation concludes would mean assuming the risk of any penalty affecting Leonard's contract. Rather than take that risk, Toronto is waiting — which means Ingram and Dick remain Raptors for now, and Leonard remains a Clipper, despite a deal that's been publicly agreed to for over two weeks.</p>
<div class="callout">
<div class="callout-icon">⚖️</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>What's actually at stake:</strong> As of this writing, the investigation has grown in scope — reportedly now also examining unreimbursed Clippers expenses and a previously unreported second endorsement deal involving Leonard. If the league finds a violation, Leonard's contract could theoretically be voided, and the Clippers could lose draft picks. The closest precedent is the 2000 Joe Smith–Minnesota Timberwolves case, where the team lost five first-round picks for a similar cap-circumvention violation (two were later reinstated on appeal).</div>
</div>
<!-- SECTION 4 -->
<h2>What Happens Next</h2>
<p>There's no announced timetable for the investigation's conclusion, and it has now run more than 10 months. Until it wraps, the Clippers, Raptors, and Leonard himself are all in limbo — a deal fully agreed to by both front offices, sitting frozen by a process neither team controls. For a league that prides itself on tightly managed player movement, it's an unusually public example of just how much power the investigation process holds over even a completed trade.</p>
